    <h1>🌾 Hay &amp; Grass — The Foundation of Horse Nutrition</h1>
    <p>Forage — hay and fresh grass — is the most important part of a horse's diet and should always be the base of what your horse eats.</p>

    <h2>🌱 Fresh Grass vs. Hay</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Fresh grass</strong> is high in water and nutrients, but too much at once (especially in spring) can cause digestive issues or laminitis</li>
        <li><strong>Hay</strong> is dried grass — more consistent in quality and easier to store</li>
        <li>Most stabled horses eat hay as their primary forage</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🌾 Types of Hay</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Timothy hay:</strong> Lower in calories — great for horses that don't need a lot of energy</li>
        <li><strong>Orchard grass hay:</strong> Higher in calories and protein — good for growing horses</li>
        <li><strong>Alfalfa (Lucerne) hay:</strong> Very high in protein — best used as a supplement</li>
        <li><strong>Meadow hay:</strong> Mixed grasses — a good all-around option</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>✅ What Good Hay Looks Like</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Green to light golden color (not brown or gray)</li>
        <li>Fresh, pleasant smell — not musty or moldy</li>
        <li>No dust, mold, or visible weeds</li>
        <li>Soft texture, not overly dry and brittle</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>❌ Signs of Bad Hay</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Black or dark brown patches — signs of mold</li>
        <li>Strong musty smell</li>
        <li>Very dusty — can cause respiratory issues</li>
        <li>Wet or clumped bales — risk of heat and mold</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🕐 How Often Should Horses Eat Hay?</h2>
    <p>Horses should have access to hay throughout the day. Leaving them without forage for more than 4 hours increases the risk of ulcers and colic. A common approach is feeding 2–3 times daily, or using a slow-feed hay net.</p>

